 formula KC4H5O6, is the potassium acid salt of tartaric acid (a carboxylic acid)—specifically, l-( + )-tartaric acid. Especially in cooking, it is also known as cream of tartar. Tartaric acid and potassium naturally occur in grapes; its crude precipitate form, tartar or wine stone, is a byproduct of winemaking found deposited on top of must barrels, which can be purified."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-cream-of-tartar"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cream_of_tartar"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Egg white","ingredient/egg-white"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}]},"ingredient/brassica-oleracea":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/brassica-oleracea","n":"Brassica oleracea","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 10 cuisines","intro":[["Brassica oleracea, also known as wild cabbage in its uncultivated form, is a plant of the family Brassicaceae."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-brassica-oleracea"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brassica_oleracea"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":10,"groups":[["Polish cuisine","cuisine/polish-cuisine",[["Borscht","dish/borscht"],["Cabbage soup","dish/cabbage-soup"]]],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine",[["Borscht","dish/borscht"],["Cabbage soup","dish/cabbage-soup"]]],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",[["Borscht","dish/borscht"],["Cabbage soup","dish/cabbage-soup"]]],["Belarusian cuisine","cuisine/belarusian-cuisine",[["Borscht","dish/borscht"]]],["Kazakh cuisine","cuisine/kazakh-cuisine",[["Dimlama","dish/dimlama"]]],["Kyrgyz cuisine","cuisine/kyrgyz-cuisine",[["Dimlama","dish/dimlama"]]],["Lithuanian cuisine","cuisine/lithuanian-cuisine",[["Borscht","dish/borscht"]]],["Moldovan cuisine","cuisine/moldovan-cuisine",[["Borscht","dish/borscht"]]],["Romanian cuisine","cuisine/romanian-cuisine",[["Borscht","dish/borscht"]]],["Uzbek cuisine","cuisine/uzbek-cuisine",[["Dimlama","dish/dimlama"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Beetroot","ingredient/beetroot"],["Beta vulgaris","ingredient/beta-vulgaris"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Sauerkraut","ingredient/sauerkraut"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Veal","ingredient/veal"]]}]},"ingredient/matzo-meal":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/matzo-meal","n":"Matzo meal","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Matzah, matzo, mazza, or maẓẓah is an unleavened flatbread that is part of Jewish cuisine and forms an integral element of the Passover festival, during which chametz (leavening agent and five grains deemed by halakha to be self-leavening) is forbidden."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-matzo-meal"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matzo_meal"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine",[["Gefilte fish","dish/gefilte-fish"]]],["Sephardic Jewish cuisine","cuisine/sephardic-jewish-cuisine",[["Fritas de prasa","dish/fritas-de-prasa"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Kosher salt","ingredient/kosher-salt"],["Leeks","ingredient/leeks"],["Potatoes","ingredient/potatoes"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Whitefish","ingredient/whitefish"]]}]},"dish/jijimi":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/jijimi","n":"Jijimi","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Korean cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Jijimi is a type of Korean stew made by boiling meat, fish or vegetables. It is thicker than guk, but thinner than jjigae. Joseon mussang sinsik yori jebeop, the first color-printed Korean cookbook published in 1924, states that \"Jijimi tastes better than guk, and jjigae tastes better than jijimi, being smaller in quantity and savorier.\""]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-jijimi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jijimi"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Maangchi","ref":"site/maangchi-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"maangchi.com","note":"Maangchi's Korean recipe site and YouTube channel, one of the best known English sources for Korean cooking.","url":"https://www.maangchi.com/","suggested":true},{"name":"Korean Bapsang","ref":"site/koreanbapsang-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"koreanbapsang.com","note":"Home-style Korean recipes from Hyosun, a Korean-American mom.","url":"https://www.koreanbapsang.com/category/recipes/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Korean cuisine","links":[["Bap (rice dish)","dish/bap-rice-dish"],["Bibim-guksu","dish/bibim-guksu"],["Bibimbap","dish/bibimbap"],["Bosintang","dish/bosintang"],["Cheonggukjang","dish/cheonggukjang"],["Danji","dish/danji"],["Doenjang","dish/doenjang"],["Garak-guksu","dish/garak-guksu"],["Gochujang","dish/gochujang"],["Gopchang","dish/gopchang"],["Han Oak","dish/han-oak"],["Hwajeon","dish/hwajeon"],["Jokbal","dish/jokbal"],["Kim Jong Grillin'","dish/kim-jong-grillin"],["Korean restaurant","dish/korean-restaurant"],["Maekjeok","dish/maekjeok"],["Maeun-tang","dish/maeun-tang"],["Makchang","dish/makchang"],["Mandu-guk","dish/mandu-guk"],["Mandu-gwa","dish/mandu-gwa"],["Maum (restaurant)","dish/maum-restaurant"],["Meat jun","dish/meat-jun"],["Meju","dish/meju"],["Morkovcha","dish/morkovcha"],["Parachute (restaurant)","dish/parachute-restaurant"],["Revelry (restaurant)","dish/revelry-restaurant"],["Scorched rice","dish/scorched-rice"],["Seon (food)"